  "mechanismSummary": "Hydroxide makes HOO⁻, which adds to boron's empty orbital. A propyl group then moves from boron to oxygen with its bonding pair as the weak O–O bond breaks (a 1,2-shift). Hydroxide adds to boron, the propoxide leaves, and water protonates it: propan-1-ol. The C–O bond forms where the C–B bond was, with the same configuration. Overall, hydroboration–oxidation adds H and OH anti-Markovnikov.",
  "references": [
    "Clayden, Greeves and Warren, Organic Chemistry, 2nd edition (Oxford University Press), chapter 19"
  ],
